In the case of a symmetrical square-wave alternating voltage, the rms value is equal to the peak value.

RMS is literally the square root of the mean of the square of the waveform.

Assume the peak voltage of the square wave is V₀.

**CALCULATION:**

 [IMAGE: https://cdn.repeatermock.com/tb/0e48cb04ca74ebb80a9237c00955b4007175f0e54fe9b8e71990c8b5f2633c35.png] 

{VRMS} = sqrt {(1)/(T){{intlimitsₜ ₌ ₀^T {[ {V( t )} ]} }²}.dt} 

{VRMS} = sqrt {(1)/(T)( {{{intlimitsₜ ₌ ₀⁽T⁾/⁽²⁾ {[ {V( t )} ]} }²}.dt + {{intlimitsₜ ₌ ₍T₎/₍₂₎^T {[ {V( t )} ]} }²}.dt} )} 

From t = 0 to (T)/(2) V(t) = + V₀

From t = (T)/(2) to T, V(t) = - V₀.

On Substituting,

{VRMS} = sqrt {(1)/(T)( {{{intlimitsₜ ₌ ₀⁽T⁾/⁽²⁾ {{V₀}²} }²}.dt + intlimitsₜ ₌ ₍T₎/₍₂₎^T {{V₀}²} .dt} )} 

{VRMS} = sqrt {frac{{{V₀}²}}{T} times T} 

{VRMS} = sqrt {{V₀}²} = {V₀}.

**__Concept__:**

The root-mean-square value or the RMS value of a periodic signal is given by:

{XRMS} = sqrt {(1)/(T)mathop smallint nolimits₀^T {{[ {X( t )} ]}²}.dt} 

**__Calculation__:**

From the figure, x(t) can be written as:

x( t ) = left{ {begin{array}{*{20}{c}} {(2)/(T) · t for 0 le t le (T)/(2)}\ {0 for (T)/(2) le t le T} end{array}} right.

The RMS value will therefore be:

{XRMS} = sqrt {(1)/(T)[ {mathop smallint nolimits₀⁽T⁾/⁽²⁾ {{( {(2)/(T)t} )}²} · dt + mathop smallint nolimits₍T₎/₍₂₎^T {{( 0 )}²} · dt} ]} 

= sqrt {(1)/(T) · (4)/({{T²)}} · [ {frac{{{t³}}}{3}} ]₀¹/²} 

= sqrt {(4)/({3{T³)}} · frac{{{T³}}}{8}}

= sqrt {(1)/(6)} Rightarrow {XRMS} = 0.408 

